{"runId":"20260919T094157-reasoning","condition":"reasoning","questionId":"q50","difficulty":10,"category":"logic","slot":"A","model":"glm-5.3-flash","expected":"3","at":"2026-09-19T09:44:49.690Z","raw":"**3 weighings.**\n\nWith 12 coins, there are 24 possible outcomes (12 coins × 2 possibilities: heavier or lighter). Each weighing has 3 possible results (left heavier, right heavier, balanced), so k weighings can distinguish at most 3^k outcomes. Since 3^2 = 9 < 24 but 3^3 = 27 ≥ 24, at least 3 weighings are needed — and a classic strategy achieves this:\n\n1. **Weighing 1:** Put coins 1,2,3,4 against 5,6,7,8.\n   - **Balanced:** The fake is among 9,10,11,12. Weigh 9,10,11 against 1,2,3 (known genuine).\n     - Balanced → coin 12 is fake; weigh 12 against any genuine coin to determine heavy/light.\n     - Left heavy → fake is among 9,10,11 and is heavy; weigh 9 vs 10 (the heavier one is fake; if balanced, 11 is fake-heavy).\n     - Left light → same logic, fake is light.\n   - **Unbalanced (say 1,2,3,4 heavy):** The fake is among these 8, and coins 9–12 are genuine. Weigh 1,2,5 against 3,4,6 (rotating coins).\n     - If left heavy: either 1 or 2 is heavy, or 6 is light → weigh 1 vs 2 (heavier is fake; if balanced, 6 is fake-light).\n     - If right heavy: either 3 or 4 is heavy, or 5 is light → weigh 3 vs 4.\n     - If balanced: the fake is among the coins removed (7 or 8, which must be light) → weigh 7 vs 8; the lighter one is fake.\n\nEvery case is resolved within 3 weighings, identifying both the coin and whether it is heavy or light.\n\nANSWER: 3","extracted":"3","correct":true,"latencyMs":46086,"promptTokens":105,"completionTokens":451,"reasoningTokens":22,"retried":false}
